<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Ms. Perón returns eight days after her first visit, as arranged, to hear her biopsy result in person; the visit is in Spanish. Before giving the result, Dr. Barragán asked what she understood so far and how she wanted to hear news. She said she suspected something serious, wanted to know everything, directly and herself, and wanted no one else in the room. She was told plainly that the biopsy shows a cancer of the cervix, an invasive squamous cell carcinoma, and that the HPV test found HPV type 16 and the cytology also showed cancer cells. After a pause and some tears she asked whether it can be cured, whether she will be able to work, and whether she could have children. Dr. Osei-Bonsu of gynecologic oncology then joined, with a professional Spanish interpreter, as Ms. Perón agreed. Since the first visit the bleeding has been lighter, she takes the iron daily with dark stools and no stomach upset, and she uses acetaminophen once or twice a day for the pelvic ache. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Element</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Result given</td><td>Invasive squamous cell carcinoma of the cervix; HPV 16 positive; cytology with cancer cells</td></tr><tr><td>How it was given</td><td>In person, in Spanish, to her alone, after asking what she wanted to know</td></tr><tr><td>Her questions</td><td>Cure, work, and children</td></tr><tr><td>Bleeding</td><td>Lighter; iron taken daily</td></tr><tr><td>Scores</td><td>Pelvic pain 4 of 10; PHQ-9 9; GAD-7 9</td></tr></tbody></table></text>

  </section>
</component>

<component>
  <section>
