<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Squamous cell carcinoma of the cervix (C53.9)</content>. Invasive squamous cell carcinoma of the cervix, HPV 16 positive, about 4.5 cm with clinical spread into the left parametrium: at least FIGO 1943 stage IIB on examination. The diagnosis was given to her first, in person and in Spanish, in the way she asked; she chose to hear all of it and to decide herself who else is told. Dr. Osei-Bonsu explained that staging with MRI and PET-CT comes next, that a tumor of this size with parametrial spread is treated with chemoradiation rather than surgery, and that this treatment aims at cure. Fertility and ovarian function were discussed before any treatment: the treatment ends the ability to carry a pregnancy and usually ends ovarian function; egg or embryo freezing would take about two weeks, the ovaries could be moved surgically out of the radiation field, and a gestational carrier would be needed for any later pregnancy. She will decide by the next visit. [S]<list><item>Pelvic MRI and FDG PET-CT within ten days.</item><item>HIV and hepatitis C tests today.</item><item>Fertility decision at the next visit, with reproductive endocrinology seen within days if she wants egg freezing.</item><item>Oncology social worker met her today; spiritual care offered.</item><item>She decides what her family and colleagues are told; no information to anyone without her consent.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Abnormal bleeding with a visible cervical mass (N93.8)</content>. Superseded by the diagnosis of cervical cancer today. [S]<list><item>Problem resolved.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Iron deficiency anemia (D50.0)</content>. Eight days into oral iron; bleeding lighter. A blood count at about one month. [S]<list><item>Continue ferrous sulfate daily.</item><item>Blood count at the next visit.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Protocol monitoring: items the monitoring protocol calls for at this visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Measure</th><th>Interval</th><th>Status</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Disclosure and information preferences: she is told first, in Spanish, as much as she wants to know; she decides who else is told</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Told in person, in Spanish, alone as she asked, after checking what she wanted to know; she asked about cure, work and children, and each question was answered</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Pelvic, speculum and rectovaginal examination</td><td>At diagnosis; every three to six months for two years after treatment</td><td>Completed today</td><td>About 4.5 cm tumor; left parametrial spread not reaching the pelvic wall; vagina and rectum free</td></tr><tr><td>Imaging</td><td>Pelvic MRI</td><td>At staging, for local extent</td><td>Ordered</td><td>Within ten days</td></tr><tr><td>Imaging</td><td>FDG PET-CT</td><td>At staging; about three months after chemoradiation</td><td>Ordered</td><td>Within ten days, for nodes and distant spread</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Kidney function (creatinine, eGFR) and magnesium for cisplatin</td><td>Before cisplatin, weekly during it, and after</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Creatinine 0.7 mg/dL, eGFR 117</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>HIV and hepatitis C tests</td><td>At diagnosis</td><td>Ordered</td><td>Drawn today with her agreement</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Blood count (hemoglobin, white cells, platelets)</td><td>About one month after starting oral iron, then every three months for a year; weekly during chemoradiation</td><td>Due</td><td>Hemoglobin 9.4 g/dL eight days ago; next at about one month</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Pelvic pain score and analgesic safety (no anti-inflammatory painkillers with cisplatin)</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>4 of 10; acetaminophen once or twice a day</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>PHQ-9 and GAD-7</td><td>At diagnosis, before treatment, at mid-treatment, at the end of treatment, and in follow-up</td><td>Completed today</td><td>PHQ-9 9 (item 9 scored 0), GAD-7 9, on the day of diagnosis</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>Fertility and ovarian function counseling and her decision</td><td>Before cancer treatment begins</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Options explained; her decision due at the next visit</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>Oncology social worker and spiritual care</td><td>At diagnosis and through treatment</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Social worker met her today; spiritual care offered</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Goal progress:</paragraph><list><item>Diagnosis and stage explained to her directly, in Spanish, with time for questions, before any treatment decision. In progress; target 1951-03-02 [S]</item><item>Hemoglobin 10 g/dL or more before radiation begins. In progress; target 1951-03-02 [S]</item><item>Chemoradiation and brachytherapy completed within eight weeks. In progress; target 1951-05-18 [S]</item><item>No evidence of disease on PET-CT and examination three months after treatment. In progress; target 1951-08-17 [S]</item><item>PHQ-9 below 5 by six months. In progress; target 1951-08-17 [S]</item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: NCCN Clinical Practice Guidelines in Oncology: Cervical Cancer (Version 1.2027); Revised FIGO staging for carcinoma of the cervix uteri (2018 (published 2019)); Patient-Clinician Communication: American Society of Clinical Oncology Consensus Guideline (2017); Fertility Preservation in Patients With Cancer: ASCO Clinical Practice Guideline Update (2018); Management of Anxiety and Depression in Adult Survivors of Cancer: ASCO Guideline Update (2023); CMID: Clinical Monitoring Intelligence Database, anemia monograph (2026)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Results of MRI, PET-CT, HIV and hepatitis C tests at a visit in about two weeks, with Dr. Osei-Bonsu, when the stage, treatment, and her fertility decision will be discussed. Next scheduled visit 1951-03-02: staging results (pelvic MRI and PET-CT), FIGO stage, and her treatment and fertility decisions before chemoradiation. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
